PG.notes("s10-display-grid", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces CSS Grid as the two-dimensional layout tool, how it differs from flexbox, and how the two combine. Shows the minimum grid syntax (display: grid, grid-template-columns/rows, gap) and ends with an exercise that builds an 8x8 chessboard.</p>\n<h3>Why Grid exists</h3>\n<ul><li>Layouts with blocks in both directions (a header, side nav, content areas, footer) are hard to build with a one-dimensional row of flex items, floats or tables. Grid is designed for these.</li><li>Real sites such as dashboards and weather pages are built from grid areas and stay proportional when resized.</li></ul>\n<h3>Grid versus flexbox</h3>\n<ul><li>Flexbox: aligns content along one line (a row or a column). Grid: places content on two axes, rows and columns, like a table structure.</li><li>Grid items snap to shared column and row lines, so gaps and edges line up. Flex items adapt and squash to fit content, so items in different rows rarely align perfectly. Choose by whether you want strict boxes or flowing content.</li><li>A 1D grid is possible, and 2D arrangements are possible with wrapping flex, but each tool behaves differently when resized.</li><li>They are complementary and commonly nested: a flex column inside a grid cell, or a grid inside a flex item. Most designs combine both, plus other tools or frameworks.</li><li>The lecture points to a side-by-side demo site (appbrewery.github.io/grid-vs-flexbox) to watch each behave while resizing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Minimum grid syntax</h3>\n<ul><li>Container: the parent gets display: grid. Its direct children become grid items. Same pattern as display: flex.</li><li>grid-template-columns defines the number and width of columns; grid-template-rows defines the rows. Values here are fr (fractional) units.</li><li>grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr makes two columns where the second is twice the width of the first; grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr makes two equal rows.</li><li>gap: 10px sets the spacing between both rows and columns, replacing margins and padding on the items.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> fr divides the free space of the container after fixed sizes are taken out; it is explained properly in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Default grid container behaviour</h3>\n<ul><li>A grid container is block-level so it tries to span the full width of its parent; its height only grows to fit the content (rows).</li><li>Items stretch to fill their cell unless width/height are set on them.</li><li>If columns are defined but rows are not, extra rows are created automatically (implicit rows) and sized to their content. This is why the chessboard already looks right with only columns defined; adding rows makes the intent explicit.</li><li>Setting a fixed width on the container (800px) stops the tracks from spreading out when the screen is wider.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Smallest useful grid: 2 columns in ratio 1:2, 2 equal rows, 10px gaps</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;\n  grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr;\n  gap: 10px;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: 10.1 Display Grid: chessboard</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html has a .container holding 64 divs, each with class white or black, alternating so that colours flip each row. No CSS sizes or colours yet (the divs are empty and invisible). goal.png shows the board.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make an 8x8 chessboard where every square is 100px by 100px, using display: grid and grid-template-columns/rows. Colours are optional (the reference uses #f0d9b5 and #b58863).</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Give .white a height and width of 100px and a background colour.</li><li>Do the same for .black with a different colour. At this point the squares appear stacked vertically because divs are block elements.</li><li>Set .container to display: grid.</li><li>Add grid-template-columns with eight 1fr tracks.</li><li>Add grid-template-rows with eight 1fr tracks for clarity.</li><li>Give .container a width of 800px so the board stays compact on wide screens.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A compact 800 x 800 board of alternating light and dark squares.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  width: 800px;\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;\n  grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;\n}\n.white {\n  height: 100px;\n  width: 100px;\n  background-color: #f0d9b5;\n}\n.black {\n  height: 100px;\n  width: 100px;\n  background-color: #b58863;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the container width: tracks spread across a wide screen and show gaps between squares.</li><li>Defining sizes on the items but not display: grid on the parent, which leaves squares stacked.</li><li>Counting the wrong number of tracks; 8 columns are needed for 8 squares per row.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Repeating 1fr eight times works but repeat(8, 1fr) is shorter; repeat() comes in the next lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s10-grid-sizing", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers every way to size grid tracks: fixed units, auto, fr, minmax(), repeat(), the grid-template shorthand, and implicit tracks via grid-auto-rows/columns. Also shows Chrome DevTools' grid overlay and finishes with an exercise that reproduces a responsive grid.</p>\n<h3>Fixed-size tracks</h3>\n<ul><li>grid-template-rows: 100px 200px and grid-template-columns: 400px 800px create exactly those track sizes. With four items you get a 2x2 grid.</li><li>Fixed tracks are not responsive: resizing the window does not change them.</li><li>rem also counts as fixed for this purpose. It scales with the root font size but not with the viewport.</li><li>grid-template is a shorthand: rows first, a slash, then columns, e.g. grid-template: 100px 200px / 400px 800px. It is compact but hides intent, so prefer the explicit longhands while learning; you will still meet it in other people's code.</li></ul>\n<h3>auto tracks</h3>\n<ul><li>A column set to auto stretches to use the remaining horizontal space of the container (the grid fills 100% of the available width). With 200px auto, the second column grows and shrinks with the window.</li><li>A row set to auto behaves differently: it only becomes as tall as its content and does not follow the viewport height.</li><li>So auto on columns = fill available space; auto on rows = fit content.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fractional units (fr)</h3>\n<ul><li>fr tracks share the available space in ratio, e.g. 1fr 2fr gives the second track double the first. Decimals and other ratios such as 2.5fr or 3fr 5fr are fine.</li><li>Columns use the available width; rows follow content height. If you add more content to one cell, its fr row grows and the others adjust to keep the ratio.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Before fr ratios are applied, fixed-size tracks are subtracted from the container's free space.</li></ul>\n<h3>minmax()</h3>\n<ul><li>minmax(min, max) lets a track flex within limits. For example a column of minmax(400px, 800px) shrinks as far as 400px and grows up to 800px, then stops. Useful to stop images or text blocks from becoming too narrow or too wide.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The repo demo uses minmax(400px, 5fr), mixing a length and an fr value, which is also valid.</li></ul>\n<h3>repeat()</h3>\n<ul><li>repeat(count, size) removes repetition: repeat(8, 1fr) replaces eight copies of 1fr, and repeat(2, 200px) replaces 200px 200px.</li><li>It produces the same layout as listing each track by hand.</li></ul>\n<h3>Explicit versus implicit tracks</h3>\n<ul><li>Items fill cells left to right, then continue on the next row, starting at the top-left.</li><li>A template larger than the number of items just leaves empty cells at the end (a 2x3 grid with four items leaves two cells empty).</li><li>If there are more items than the template provides, extra items go into automatically created tracks. New rows are as tall as their content; they reuse the existing column widths.</li><li>grid-auto-rows and grid-auto-columns set the size of these automatically created tracks, e.g. grid-auto-rows: 300px. Handy when items are added dynamically.</li></ul>\n<h3>Chrome DevTools grid overlay</h3>\n<ul><li>Open DevTools (right-click, Inspect, or the three-dot menu, More tools). Elements with display: grid show a 'grid' badge in the Elements panel; clicking it toggles an overlay.</li><li>The Layout tab lets you change the overlay colour and toggle line numbers, track sizes and extended lines.</li><li>Overlay track-size labels show the real size of each column and row, which helps to debug sizing.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Fixed, auto, fr and minmax tracks side by side</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* fixed */\n.a { display: grid; grid-template-rows: 100px 200px; grid-template-columns: 400px 800px; }\n/* shorthand: rows / columns */\n.b { display: grid; grid-template: 100px 200px / 400px 800px; }\n/* auto: column fills, row fits content */\n.c { display: grid; grid-template-rows: 100px auto; grid-template-columns: 200px auto; }\n/* fractions */\n.d { display: grid; grid-template-rows: 1fr 2fr; grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr; }\n/* limits */\n.e { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 200px minmax(400px, 800px); }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">repeat() and implicit rows (from the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-rows: repeat(2, 200px);\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 200px);\n  grid-auto-rows: 300px; /* the 5th item gets a new 300px row */\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Grid Sizing test: match the green grid</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The 'Test' page of the course's grid-sizing demo site: a CSS text box with 'Apply CSS', a green target grid (.parent) and a purple grid you control. Size hints are written in the cells. Only CSS for the grid container may be written.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the purple grid look and resize like the green one.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Set display: grid on the container.</li><li>Rows: the first two are equal and the third is twice as tall, so grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr 2fr.</li><li>Columns: first expands to fill space (auto), second is fixed (400px), third has limits (minmax(200px, 500px)).</li><li>The extra last row is 50px high; set grid-auto-rows: 50px rather than adding a fourth explicit row.</li><li>Click Apply CSS, then resize the window to check the responsive behaviour matches. If nothing works, check spelling, e.g. the 's' in grid-template-rows and -columns.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Same layout as the green grid at any width: first column flexes, second stays 400px, third flexes between 200 and 500px; the fourth row stays 50px.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.parent {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr 2fr;\n  grid-template-columns: auto 400px minmax(200px, 500px);\n  grid-auto-rows: 50px;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Typos such as grid-template-row (missing s); the property is silently ignored.</li><li>Expecting auto rows to fill the viewport height; they only fit content.</li><li>Assuming rem-sized tracks respond to viewport size.</li><li>Defining too few explicit tracks and being surprised by content-sized implicit rows; set grid-auto-rows.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The repo's footers ('Made with ...') are not part of the lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s10-grid-placement", "<p class=\"summary\">Defines the vocabulary of CSS Grid (container, items, tracks, cells, lines) and then shows how to place items explicitly with grid-column, grid-row, grid-column-start/end, order and the grid-area shorthand, including overlapping items. Three small Flexbox/Grid exercises and the Grid Garden game follow.</p>\n<h3>Grid vocabulary</h3>\n<ul><li>Grid container: the parent element (usually a div) with display: grid. Grid items: its direct children.</li><li>Tracks: the rows and columns collectively. Row tracks and column tracks are created and sized with grid-template-rows and grid-template-columns.</li><li>Grid cell: the smallest unit, sitting at the intersection of one row track and one column track. Cells can differ in size. One grid item can occupy several cells (spanning two columns, or two rows).</li><li>Grid lines: the dividing lines between tracks, horizontal and vertical. They are layout-only: the only way to affect them is the gap property (their thickness). They cannot be coloured or hold content.</li><li>These terms (container, item, track, cell, line) are used throughout the rest of the grid material, especially for placement.</li></ul>\n<h3>Base setup used in the demos</h3>\n<ul><li>A .container div holds three .item divs (cowboy, astronaut, book emojis). Container CSS: display: grid, height: 100vh, gap: 3rem, grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1.5fr, grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr.</li><li>A div is full window width by default but only as tall as its content, so height: 100vh stretches the container to the bottom of the viewport.</li><li>Without any placement rules, items auto-flow into empty cells in source order, one per cell, left to right then top to bottom. With three items and six cells, the second row is left empty.</li><li>gap: 3rem makes every grid line 3rem thick, so no margin or padding is needed to separate items.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> gap is shorthand for row-gap and column-gap; give two values to set them separately.</li></ul>\n<h3>Centring item content with Flexbox (exercise 1)</h3>\n<ul><li>Each .item is made a flex container: display: flex, justify-content: center (main axis, horizontal for a row) and align-items: center (cross axis, vertical).</li><li>Grid and Flexbox are complementary: grid lays out the page-level areas, flexbox aligns the content inside each area.</li><li>The same centring would be painful with floats or absolute positioning.</li></ul>\n<h3>Spanning with grid-column and grid-row</h3>\n<ul><li>grid-column: span 2 on the cowboy item makes it start where auto-placement puts it and cover two column tracks.</li><li>Chrome DevTools can overlay the grid: click the grid badge next to the container in the Elements panel to show lines and their numbers. The overlay colour is changeable, and negative numbers are displayed too.</li><li>Lines are numbered from 1: with three columns there are four vertical lines (1, 2, 3, 4). Counting from the end, the last line is -1, then -2, -3, -4.</li><li>grid-column is shorthand for grid-column-start and grid-column-end. Example: grid-column-start: 2; grid-column-end: 4 covers the second and third columns.</li><li>-1 always means the last line and 1 the first line, which is handy when the number of columns is unknown (for example generated dynamically). Prefer 1 and -1; -2 or -3 get confusing to read.</li><li>The start and end can be given in either order. Start at line 4 and end at line 2 still covers the same two columns because the browser swaps them.</li><li>Values can mix span and auto: start can be span 2 with end auto. grid-column: span 2 is equivalent to a start of auto plus a span of 2.</li><li>grid-row works identically on the vertical axis with grid-row-start and grid-row-end.</li></ul>\n<h3>The order property</h3>\n<ul><li>A second item given grid-column: span 2 after the cowboy has no room on the first row, so auto-placement leaves it on its default track and the layout looks wrong.</li><li>Grid items support order just as flex items do. Default is 0 for all items. Giving the astronaut order: 1 pushes it after all items with a lower order, so it moves to the end (bottom row).</li><li>Only relative values matter: 1 or 100 behaves the same if everything else is 0. Items with higher values go later, ordering proceeds left to right then top to bottom.</li><li>Exercise 2: with order: 1 already set, add grid-column: span 2 to the astronaut so it sits beneath the blue div.</li></ul>\n<h3>grid-area shorthand</h3>\n<ul><li>Instead of order, an item can be given exact coordinates: grid-column-start 1, grid-column-end 3, grid-row-start 2, grid-row-end 3.</li><li>grid-area packs these into one declaration of four slash-separated values in this order: row-start / column-start / row-end / column-end. Example: grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 3.</li><li>The value is a set of line numbers, and the end line is exclusive, so 3 as end line means stop at line 3, not including track 3.</li><li>If one item uses grid-area, the others should too for predictable behaviour, because auto-placed items fill around explicitly placed ones.</li><li>Exercise 3: make the book fill the third column across both rows. Options: grid-row: span 2; or grid-area: 1 / 3 / 3 / 4; or with negative lines grid-area: 1 / 3 / -1 / -1.</li></ul>\n<h3>Overlapping items</h3>\n<ul><li>Unlike Flexbox, grid lets items share cells and layer on top of each other.</li><li>Example: book with grid-area: 1 / 2 / 3 / 4 spans from row 1 column 2 to row 3 column 4, so it sits across the cowboy and astronaut boxes.</li><li>Use an 8-digit hex colour for see-through effects. The last two hex digits are alpha; 80 is roughly 50% opacity (e.g. #e5833180).</li><li>Later items in source order paint over earlier ones.</li></ul>\n<h3>Practice game</h3>\n<ul><li>Grid Garden (appbrewery.github.io/gridgarden), by the author of Flexbox Froggy, has 28 levels of CSS grid practice. The course recommends completing all of them before the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Base container and items, plus Flexbox centring</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  height: 100vh;\n  display: grid;\n  gap: 3rem;\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1.5fr;\n  grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr;\n}\n\n.item {\n  font-size: 5rem;\n  color: white;\n  background-color: blueviolet;\n  display: flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Three equivalent ways to say the cowboy spans two columns</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.cowboy { grid-column: span 2; }\n/* same result using lines */\n.cowboy { grid-column-start: 1; grid-column-end: 3; }\n.cowboy { grid-column: 1 / 3; }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Negative line numbers and reversed start/end</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.cowboy { grid-column: 2 / -1; }  /* line 2 to the last line */\n.cowboy { grid-column: 4 / 2; }   /* reversed, still columns 2-3 */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">order moves the astronaut to the end; then span fills two columns</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.astronaut {\n  background-color: #03989e;\n  order: 1;\n  grid-column: span 2;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final layout with grid-area (row-start / column-start / row-end / column-end)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.cowboy    { background-color: #00b9ff; grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 3; }\n.astronaut { background-color: #03989e; grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 3; }\n.book      { background-color: #e5833180; grid-area: 1 / 2 / 3 / 4; } /* overlaps both */</pre>\n<h2>The task: 10.3 Grid Placement: three exercises</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download the lesson zip (exercise1.html to exercise3.html, goal1-3.png, demo1-4.html, solution1-3.html). Each exercise has the cowboy/astronaut/book container with grid-template-columns 1fr 1fr 1.5fr and two 1fr rows.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> 1: centre each emoji in its box with Flexbox. 2: place the astronaut under the cowboy spanning two columns. 3: make the book orange box fill the whole third column (two rows).</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Exercise 1: on .item add display: flex, justify-content: center and align-items: center so each emoji is centred both ways.</li><li>Open demo1.html to see the cowboy rule (background #00b9ff, grid-column: span 2) and inspect it with the DevTools grid overlay.</li><li>Exercise 2: in exercise2.html the astronaut already has order: 1 (see demo2.html). Add grid-column: span 2 so it takes the two left columns on the bottom row, matching goal2.png.</li><li>Optionally replace order with grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 3 (demo3.html) and observe the same result.</li><li>Exercise 3: in the book rule add grid-row: span 2 (or grid-area: 1 / 3 / 3 / 4) so the orange box fills column three on both rows, matching goal3.png.</li><li>Open demo4.html to see the overlapping variant with a semi-transparent book.</li><li>Play all 28 levels of Grid Garden.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Emojis centred in each cell; blue box across the top two columns; green box across the bottom two columns; orange box filling the right column over both rows (or overlapping the others in demo 4).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.item {\n  display: flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n}\n\n.cowboy {\n  background-color: #00b9ff;\n  grid-column: span 2;\n}\n\n.astronaut {\n  background-color: #03989e;\n  grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 3;\n}\n\n.book {\n  background-color: #e58331;\n  grid-area: 1 / 3 / 3 / 4;\n  /* or grid-area: 1 / 3 / -1 / -1; or grid-row: span 2; */\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting grid-area values in the wrong order. It is row-start / column-start / row-end / column-end, the reverse of the usual x then y thinking.</li><li>Forgetting that the end line is where the item stops, not the last track it covers: spanning columns 1 and 2 needs end line 3.</li><li>Giving an item span 2 when there is no room left on the current row, so auto-placement drops it elsewhere.</li><li>Mixing auto-placed items with a few explicitly placed ones and being surprised by the result.</li><li>Using -2, -3 and similar negative lines, which are hard to read at a glance.</li><li>Missing alpha digits when expecting overlap to show through (use 8-digit hex or rgba).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo files carry a personal footer element on each demo page; ignore it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s10-mondrian", "<p class=\"summary\">Section project: rebuild a Piet Mondrian composition as a to-scale web page using CSS Grid, with the container centred in the window by Flexbox. The second half walks through the sample solution: nine divs, fixed-pixel tracks, a black background showing through the gap, and a few span/grid-area placements.</p>\n<h3>Project brief</h3>\n<ul><li>Recreate Mondrian's Composition No. III (red, blue, yellow and black blocks on white) using only CSS grid. A dimensions image gives measured sizes for every block, and index.html lists the hex colours in a comment.</li><li>Optional: pick a different Mondrian composition (image search 'Mondrian compositions') and judge track sizes by eye, or do several.</li><li>The starter has no HTML body and no CSS written; everything is up to the learner. The goal page is centred in the window.</li><li>Skills being reviewed: sizing tracks, placing items with span / grid-area, and centring with Flexbox.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the sample solution is built</h3>\n<ul><li>One div per rectangle in the painting: nine divs inside a single grid container. Divs that differ in colour get an extra class (red, blue, yellow, black); the rest share a white class.</li><li>The container gets a fixed width and height matching the dimensions image (748px square), display: grid and a black background-color.</li><li>The black lines are not borders: grid lines cannot be coloured, so the container's black background shows through the gap. A gap of 9px therefore produces the thick lines.</li><li>Tracks use fixed pixel sizes taken from the measurements. Four columns (320px 198px 153px 50px) and four rows (414px 130px 155px 22px). Some blocks are made of two column tracks, which is why there are four columns rather than three.</li><li>Every item defaults to the off-white background (#f0f1ec); the coloured ones override it.</li><li>Placement: the wide white block uses grid-column: span 3. A tall white block uses grid-row: span 2. A square white block uses grid-area: 2 / 2 / 4 / 4 (row-start / column-start / row-end / column-end) because that was easier to express as coordinates.</li><li>To centre the painting on screen, the body is a flex container with justify-content: center, align-items: center and height: 100vh (margin 0).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Sample solution CSS (colours in the starter: red #E72F24, black #232629, blue #004592, yellow #F9D01E, white #f0f1ec)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.container {\n  height: 748px;\n  width: 748px;\n  display: grid;\n  background-color: #000;\n  grid-template-columns: 320px 198px 153px 50px;\n  grid-template-rows: 414px 130px 155px 22px;\n  gap: 9px;\n}\n\n.item { background-color: #f0f1ec; }\n.red { background-color: #e72f24; }\n.white1 { grid-column: span 3; }\n.white2 { grid-row: span 2; }\n.white3 { grid-area: 2 / 2 / 4 / 4; }\n.blue { background-color: #004592; border-bottom: 10px solid #000; }\n.white4 { grid-row: span 2; }\n.yellow { background-color: #f9d01e; }\n.black { background-color: #232629; }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">HTML: nine items in source order</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item red&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item white1&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item white2&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item white3&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item blue&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item white4&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item white5&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item yellow&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item black&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 10.4 Mondrian Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter folder with an empty index.html (colour hex codes in a comment), dimensions.png showing every measurement, goal.png and solution.html.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build a to-scale Mondrian painting with CSS grid, centred on the screen.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Read the dimensions image and count the distinct column and row boundaries; one block spanning two column widths means extra tracks.</li><li>Write one div per block inside a grid container. Add classes for the coloured ones.</li><li>Give the container a fixed width/height, display: grid, a black background and a gap sized for the black lines.</li><li>Define grid-template-columns and grid-template-rows with the measured pixel sizes.</li><li>Set default white, then override with red, blue, yellow and black colours.</li><li>Position blocks that cover several tracks with grid-column / grid-row span or grid-area.</li><li>Centre the container in the viewport using Flexbox on the body (height: 100vh).</li><li>Compare with solution.html.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A 748px square painting with thick black lines from the gap, centred in the window, matching goal.png.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.container {\n  width: 748px; height: 748px;\n  display: grid;\n  background-color: #000;\n  grid-template-columns: 320px 198px 153px 50px;\n  grid-template-rows: 414px 130px 155px 22px;\n  gap: 9px;\n}\n.white1 { grid-column: span 3; }\n.white2, .white4 { grid-row: span 2; }\n.white3 { grid-area: 2 / 2 / 4 / 4; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Trying to colour the grid lines directly; use the container background plus gap.</li><li>Forgetting body margin: 0 and height: 100vh, so the centring is off.</li><li>Miscounting tracks: a block made of two widths needs two columns, so there are four columns rather than three.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference repo, index.html is a student's finished solution rather than a blank starter, with a slightly different layout from solution.html. Its blue box has a border-bottom, an odd extra that the sample also has. The footer line in both files is the author's personal addition; ignore it.</li></ul>");
